# platform.linux.storage.bus

The emulated board's mm.mcu platform over the virtual SD card and the virtual
SPI flash, which share SPI 0 as rp2040_geek's TF socket and a flash chip
beside it would:

Opening fence
SPI 0         clock GPIO 18, transmit GPIO 19, receive GPIO 20
SD card       chip select GPIO 23
SPI flash     chip select GPIO 17
Closing fence

Every GPIO write latches, and both chip selects start high, as their pull-ups
hold them. A chip whose select is high ignores the bus and leaves the receive
line high, so the two share it as real devices do; a byte clocked with both
selected is answered 0xFF and counted as a wiring error. A configuration
naming other pins or another instance is BadArgument. Time is the host's
steady clock, and a delay sleeps. Every other facility answers Unsupported.

It registers nothing: platform.linux.storage.device registers one over image
files as mm.mcu, and tests make their own over in-memory stores and select
them with mm::mcu::set_platform. A chip given as null is absent, and its
select line goes nowhere.
